1
00:00:02,305 --> 00:00:16,694
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the question that the entire Buddhist teaching is really addressing is the question of what leads to suffering and what leads to non-suffering.

2
00:00:16,714 --> 00:00:24,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, what leads to long-lasting welfare and happiness, basically, as opposed to what leads to long-lasting harm and suffering.

3
00:00:26,140 --> 00:00:27,761
[SPEAKER_00]: At least, that's how one sort of puts it.

4
00:00:30,593 --> 00:00:35,476
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not this really abstruse, complex, philosophical question.

5
00:00:35,496 --> 00:00:36,977
[SPEAKER_00]: It's apparently a basic question.

6
00:00:37,177 --> 00:00:45,721
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't know the answer for yourself to that question, then there's kind of no point in asking anything else.

7
00:00:46,722 --> 00:00:55,126
[SPEAKER_00]: Or anything else you're asking or doing should really be for the sake of gaining that understanding for yourself first.

8
00:01:00,156 --> 00:01:02,258
[SPEAKER_00]: That's also pretty much the definition of right view.

9
00:01:02,378 --> 00:01:10,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Having knowledge, internal clarity and knowledge for yourself in regards to what leads to suffering and what leads to non-suffering.

10
00:01:11,145 --> 00:01:22,854
[SPEAKER_00]: If you really admit, I don't know, but there is the possibility of not knowing for myself what leads to the end of suffering, then that means that I'm responsible for not doing it.

11
00:01:23,334 --> 00:01:27,778
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if I don't know, that's only because I haven't made the necessary effort

12
00:01:28,418 --> 00:01:30,262
[SPEAKER_00]: To gain that knowledge for myself.

13
00:01:31,565 --> 00:01:38,039
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, it's kind of unpleasant to admit that and feel the weight of that responsibility.

14
00:01:40,112 --> 00:02:00,794
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, if you do admit that then, if you recognize that you don't know or that your knowledge is at least severely limited or incomplete in regard to what leads to long-lasting welfare and happiness versus what leads to long-term harm and suffering, and you recognize there is a possibility of knowing, gaining that right view,

15
00:02:03,393 --> 00:02:08,197
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, so how should you... what can you do to start gaining it?

16
00:02:08,257 --> 00:02:12,181
[SPEAKER_00]: To start getting that right clarity and understanding?

17
00:02:12,901 --> 00:02:13,622
[SPEAKER_00]: How should you think?

18
00:02:14,523 --> 00:02:15,303
[SPEAKER_00]: And how should you act?

19
00:02:15,784 --> 00:02:17,245
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't want to just give a list.

20
00:02:17,285 --> 00:02:24,791
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, there are specific things, and I'm going to come to them in a minute, that... things that you can do, like behaviors, actions.

21
00:02:25,392 --> 00:02:28,335
[SPEAKER_00]: But what ultimately really leads to...

22
00:02:30,076 --> 00:02:37,959
[SPEAKER_00]: The key to long-lasting welfare and happiness, and to the end of suffering, is knowing that for yourself.

23
00:02:38,119 --> 00:02:42,000
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not having to depend on somebody to tell you what specific things to do.

24
00:02:45,041 --> 00:02:51,644
[SPEAKER_00]: And actually, that's also how you can start to understand your non-understanding, which is an important thing to do.

25
00:02:51,684 --> 00:02:55,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can think about the fact that you don't

26
00:02:57,170 --> 00:03:06,976
[SPEAKER_00]: What does it mean that I don't know for myself what leads to end of suffering or how to put an end to suffering?

27
00:03:07,617 --> 00:03:24,888
[SPEAKER_00]: You can basically see the limit to your own knowledge wherever there is anxiety or wherever there's possibility for like anxiety or fear or this kind of deep doubt and uncertainty about the nature of things to be there.

28
00:03:29,121 --> 00:03:48,257
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's already sort of revealing the basic nature or structure of things that is the reason for that anxiety, or the reason for that anxiety is lack of understanding in regard to that nature of things.

29
00:03:49,578 --> 00:03:51,219
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, like, I'm saying that because

30
00:03:54,215 --> 00:03:58,958
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a way of basically already directly for yourself, without referring to what somebody else has told you.

31
00:03:58,998 --> 00:04:05,001
[SPEAKER_00]: You can recognize, okay, I don't know for myself what leads to the end of suffering.

32
00:04:05,161 --> 00:04:10,504
[SPEAKER_00]: And I know that I don't know, because I get anxious and because I'm afraid.

33
00:04:11,024 --> 00:04:12,665
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, okay, why am I anxious?

34
00:04:12,705 --> 00:04:14,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Or what makes me anxious and afraid?

35
00:04:15,006 --> 00:04:20,990
[SPEAKER_00]: You can recognize that, like, really fundamentally, the reason for that, for any kind of anxiety or fear,

36
00:04:23,091 --> 00:04:40,346
[SPEAKER_00]: You can see that your possibility of managing your circumstances or your life in ways that make you happy, basically, or that maintain your well-being, maintain your happiness, or that avoid suffering,

37
00:04:40,905 --> 00:04:47,090
[SPEAKER_00]: Anxiety is itself basically contained within certain things that you have no possibility of controlling or managing.

38
00:04:47,690 --> 00:04:49,892
[SPEAKER_00]: So... and that's why there's anxiety.

39
00:04:50,052 --> 00:05:04,343
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, you might be able today to, you know, go to work, to get it, you know, have... maintain your living, maintain your family, get enough food and so on, but all of those things are only possible

40
00:05:04,883 --> 00:05:13,869
[SPEAKER_00]: Because your body is healthy, because things are set up in a certain way that is working, thus there's peace in your in your in your country, hopefully.

41
00:05:14,749 --> 00:05:20,953
[SPEAKER_00]: All of those things can change, and are changing for somebody constantly.

42
00:05:20,973 --> 00:05:25,116
[SPEAKER_00]: At any time, at any moment, somebody is losing things on a very fundamental level.

43
00:05:25,136 --> 00:05:34,182
[SPEAKER_00]: This means they're losing even the possibility of, you know, doing whatever they can to maintain their own their own happiness and avoid suffering.

44
00:05:39,480 --> 00:06:06,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Ultimately, for everybody, no matter how fortunate their circumstances happen to be in their lives, that applies, because no matter what you do, your life is going to end in kind of a dead end, or literally a dead end, the ending of death, which really undermines, if you think about it, anything that you can do on a particular level to maintain well-being or to avoid suffering.

45
00:06:10,302 --> 00:06:14,323
[SPEAKER_00]: I was trying to think of like an analogy for this whole sort of situation.

46
00:06:14,383 --> 00:06:28,627
[SPEAKER_00]: The best one I could come up with was that it's a bit like you're being held, confined in a prison cell, that's with a guard, a prison guard in charge of it.

47
00:06:29,828 --> 00:06:29,968
[SPEAKER_00]: And

48
00:06:32,588 --> 00:06:44,134
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can ask the guard to give you things in your prison cell, like give you food, give you things to do, give you whatever you might want to ask him for.

49
00:06:45,394 --> 00:06:53,178
[SPEAKER_00]: And he, if he's in a good mood, if he's well disposed towards you, he gives them to you, basically.

50
00:06:53,258 --> 00:06:59,961
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the guard is your means of accessing everything that you have in this prison cell.

51
00:07:03,940 --> 00:07:06,742
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's up to the guard, whether he gives them to you or not.

52
00:07:07,282 --> 00:07:17,090
[SPEAKER_00]: And ultimately, he's the one holding you in that prison cell, and he's not holding you there with your happiness in mind.

53
00:07:17,150 --> 00:07:19,972
[SPEAKER_00]: He's holding there, as a prison guard, to keep you prisoner.

54
00:07:20,452 --> 00:07:22,594
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't have your best interests at heart.

55
00:07:23,034 --> 00:07:42,330
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what this basically represents is like the prison cell is kind of your entire life, your situation, with the guard there representing basically your body and senses that are kind of your means of accessing anything in particular or doing anything in particular in your life.

56
00:07:43,090 --> 00:07:49,913
[SPEAKER_00]: Or any particular pleasures that you might get, you can only get them on account of your body and your senses.

57
00:07:50,433 --> 00:07:56,516
[SPEAKER_00]: Which, as long as they're working, and as long as they're kind of treating you well, it goes okay.

58
00:07:57,376 --> 00:08:07,721
[SPEAKER_00]: But what people... what you forget, or what's pleasant to not think about, is the fact that you're very...

59
00:08:08,661 --> 00:08:18,934
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, your very ability to access anything in particular that might make you happy, or to avoid anything that might make you suffer, has to go through that body.

60
00:08:18,994 --> 00:08:22,639
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, it's again, like in that analogy,

61
00:08:24,040 --> 00:08:27,102
[SPEAKER_00]: The guard, at any moment, it's up to him.

62
00:08:28,283 --> 00:08:30,184
[SPEAKER_00]: At any moment, he might just not show up.

63
00:08:30,484 --> 00:08:41,130
[SPEAKER_00]: He might just... he might decide to not give you the thing that you ask for, or he might decide to throw... to throw horrible things to you inside the prison cell instead of pleasant things.

64
00:08:42,432 --> 00:09:03,517
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if he's nice to you most of the time, if somebody is very lucky, it's still ultimately not a good situation and it's not good to depend on that guard and depend on the things in the prison cell for your long-term welfare and happiness.

65
00:09:03,817 --> 00:09:10,879
[SPEAKER_00]: That's really where the relevance of virtue and sense restraint come in, in regard to even being able to

66
00:09:13,940 --> 00:09:17,504
[SPEAKER_00]: To see what's for your long-term welfare and happiness.

67
00:09:19,826 --> 00:09:28,994
[SPEAKER_00]: And we talk a lot about that, obviously, but it's not just this this random prerequisite, virtue and sense restraint, so that you can get the right view.

68
00:09:29,334 --> 00:09:34,879
[SPEAKER_00]: It's very directly relevant to this question of what's for your long-lasting welfare and happiness.

69
00:09:37,681 --> 00:09:37,801
[SPEAKER_00]: And

70
00:09:41,623 --> 00:10:10,965
[SPEAKER_00]: And that should be... if you've been thinking about things in the way that I've just outlined, it should already start to make sense why developing virtue, for example, would be more along the lines of what would be for your welfare and happiness, rather than depending on specific things in your life that you hold dear or that bring you joy.

71
00:10:13,281 --> 00:10:36,647
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's because while circumstances changing or can basically can take away or can can destroy literally anything in particular that you can think of in your life that that you would hold dear

72
00:10:41,688 --> 00:10:47,732
[SPEAKER_00]: Virtue is not something that can be just taken away in that sense, if it's being developed properly along the right lines.

73
00:10:49,313 --> 00:10:51,054
[SPEAKER_00]: That's that aggregate of virtue.

74
00:10:55,880 --> 00:11:06,723
[SPEAKER_00]: is something that is basically more worthy to to develop and to depend on than things like your body or anything that depends on your body.

75
00:11:07,644 --> 00:11:11,325
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what I mean by developing the aggregate of virtue as well, it's not just

76
00:11:11,765 --> 00:11:16,127
[SPEAKER_00]: It would start, obviously, by taking on the basic precepts.

77
00:11:16,727 --> 00:11:20,749
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, practicing at least the five precepts.

78
00:11:21,549 --> 00:11:23,610
[SPEAKER_00]: And, ideally, celibacy is the third.

79
00:11:24,370 --> 00:11:32,633
[SPEAKER_00]: But also, then, on top of that, it means having an attitude of just trying to avoid any fault in your actions.

80
00:11:33,174 --> 00:11:38,936
[SPEAKER_00]: So, avoiding any intentions, actions that are rooted in greed, aversion, delusion, distraction.

81
00:11:41,377 --> 00:11:51,244
[SPEAKER_00]: We all kind of know the things that shouldn't be done, and that... so you shouldn't... so what you know shouldn't be done, you don't do it, when it's rooted in a... in an unwholesome intention.

82
00:11:53,959 --> 00:12:13,028
[SPEAKER_00]: So, prioritizing this generally in your life, prioritizing the development of that virtue every day and working on that, and having that as something that is really your priority, as opposed to any other particular goal that you might be trying to achieve,

83
00:12:14,049 --> 00:12:32,085
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already, even if you're not specifically thinking about it, and even if you're not specifically aiming to practice the Buddha's teaching, that's already going to train your mind to be more content, to have more strength, to have more resilience.

84
00:12:33,827 --> 00:12:39,272
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can see why that is, because it's like

85
00:12:41,210 --> 00:12:55,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever might happen on a particular level, even if you lose your health or you lose particular things, that can't remove your virtue.

86
00:12:55,735 --> 00:13:00,017
[SPEAKER_00]: It can't make you start to be dishonest or start to...

87
00:13:00,837 --> 00:13:02,418
[SPEAKER_00]: To act out of ill will and so on.

88
00:13:02,779 --> 00:13:16,610
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's kind of an important thing to recognize even in itself, because even if you follow that reflection through to its conclusion, that's already showing you something important, which is really like the line between where your responsibility begins and where it ends.

89
00:13:16,650 --> 00:13:26,137
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, even if even if your own family is attacked, that doesn't make you and doesn't justify you then attacking somebody else.

90
00:13:26,217 --> 00:13:27,478
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a completely different thing.

91
00:13:31,421 --> 00:13:46,287
[SPEAKER_00]: And just as you can't guarantee, no matter what you do, you can't guarantee that your body will stay healthy, but even ill health can't make you unvirtuous if you've been training your mind and training your behavior in that aggregate of virtue.

92
00:13:47,107 --> 00:13:55,311
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, that's why, like you can see, virtue is more worthy to depend on than your body.

93
00:13:58,005 --> 00:14:05,654
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's also why it's like, as the suttas put it, it's a source of blameless joy.

94
00:14:06,175 --> 00:14:10,320
[SPEAKER_00]: When that aggregate of virtue is developed correctly, it's...

95
00:14:12,841 --> 00:14:29,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Like they give that simile of a king who has defeated his enemies, and he sees no danger from any direction, because he sees... somebody who's developed this aggregate of virtue would see and know that nothing that can happen can take away that aggregate of virtue.

96
00:14:29,505 --> 00:14:31,505
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just... it's there, it's been developed.

97
00:14:31,925 --> 00:14:37,247
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing can make them act out of... act out of ill-will, act out of greed.

98
00:14:42,825 --> 00:14:52,009
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's also, actually, by the way, why it's... why that is pretty much developing the composure of the mind.

99
00:14:52,449 --> 00:15:03,534
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a composure in the sense of literally like something that contains anything that might happen or change on a particular level day to day.

100
00:15:03,574 --> 00:15:11,498
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever might happen, the mind will become kind of immovable in it.

101
00:15:12,378 --> 00:15:26,957
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's literally because it has been developed along these lines of recognizing its own responsibility in regards to other things that other people might do or that might change on a particular level.

102
00:15:28,038 --> 00:15:37,221
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, it's containing you in the face of all the disturbance that might... that happens in your body, in your life, in everything else.

103
00:15:38,062 --> 00:15:56,008
[SPEAKER_00]: That's also why... that develops the capacity for your mind to be able to start contemplating and recognizing the nature of things, that kind of deep-rooted anxiety that I mentioned earlier, and what that is rooted in.

104
00:15:57,769 --> 00:16:19,471
[SPEAKER_00]: That basically means, when you then start to contemplate the nature of impermanence and change in general and things that would make you deeply anxious and afraid, it means that you're able to do so without just kind of falling apart and needing to shut your eyes to it and resort to some sensual pleasure or something to forget about it.

105
00:16:20,592 --> 00:16:23,735
[SPEAKER_00]: You have something to fall back on, to resort to, to contain you.

106
00:16:24,175 --> 00:16:44,591
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, if you think about that simile that I mentioned, eh... that I mentioned earlier about this, eh... the guard and the prison cell, sensuality basically means, eh... taking the... the particular things that you can get through the guard as your... as a source of... of happiness, basically.

107
00:16:45,151 --> 00:17:02,005
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can see immediately how that is completely the opposite to anything that would allow you to recognize the nature of that situation for what it is, because it's literally just rooted in ignoring the situation.

108
00:17:02,585 --> 00:17:05,828
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, quickly, guard, give me something.

109
00:17:07,469 --> 00:17:12,110
[SPEAKER_00]: Give me something that I can play with, that I can look at, that I can enjoy, that I can take pleasure in.

110
00:17:13,511 --> 00:17:18,533
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically just kind of fueling your sense of being in control of the prison.

111
00:17:19,433 --> 00:17:28,356
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you think you're the king of the prison, just because you can keep asking the guard for something and he gives it to you.

112
00:17:28,376 --> 00:17:34,719
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like kind of helping you to ignore that fundamental lack of ownership and lack of control.

113
00:17:36,745 --> 00:17:44,871
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as long as you're keeping doing that, it's completely impossible to actually recognize the situation for what it is.

114
00:17:45,532 --> 00:17:50,896
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's also making you, obviously, more and more dependent on those on the guard.

115
00:17:51,696 --> 00:17:55,919
[SPEAKER_00]: It's putting you more and more under his command and under his control.

116
00:17:56,760 --> 00:17:59,101
[SPEAKER_00]: The more you depend on the things that he can give you,

117
00:18:00,522 --> 00:18:08,027
[SPEAKER_00]: The more he has the capacity to hurt you, and the more easily he can hurt you.

118
00:18:08,067 --> 00:18:15,632
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why he can start torturing you, harming you and killing you, as the body does in the end.

119
00:18:20,043 --> 00:18:29,253
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why nobody who develops the right view can possibly maintain that valuing of sensuality.

120
00:18:30,354 --> 00:18:31,315
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's also why

121
00:18:34,708 --> 00:18:44,279
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensual pleasure, really, from that point of view of what actually leads to long-term welfare and happiness, as opposed to harm and suffering.

122
00:18:45,020 --> 00:18:49,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality is a source of blameworthy happiness, if you want to put it in that way.

123
00:18:49,905 --> 00:18:52,608
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, really, ultimately, it's not something pleasant.

124
00:18:52,648 --> 00:18:54,170
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not something that is

125
00:18:56,506 --> 00:19:00,588
[SPEAKER_00]: Worthy to... worthy to rely on or worthy to engage in.

126
00:19:01,649 --> 00:19:08,192
[SPEAKER_00]: And unrestraint, on the other hand, is actually... is much more pleasant from that point of view.

127
00:19:09,093 --> 00:19:15,156
[SPEAKER_00]: That composed and contained mind has the capacity to see things clearly as it... as they are.

128
00:19:15,176 --> 00:19:16,937
[SPEAKER_00]: I think there's a sutta that says that as well.

129
00:19:17,157 --> 00:19:21,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, it's possible to contemplate those same thoughts that would...

130
00:19:23,220 --> 00:19:30,828
[SPEAKER_00]: That would make you very anxious, or afraid, or that are kind of pertaining to where that deep-rooted anxiety is.

131
00:19:32,498 --> 00:19:56,328
[SPEAKER_00]: I start to contemplate them accurately, which basically means contemplating the nature of your situation accurately, without turning away from it, without finding another way to ignore it, without kind of resorting to some method or management thing to just calm your mind down.

132
00:19:59,073 --> 00:20:22,542
[SPEAKER_00]: But just to basically hold it there until you can start to see why the reason for that anxiety is not in the thing, and it's not in the way that things are, but is in your own kind of gratuitous resistance or attitude, wrong attitude, in regard to the way that things are.

133
00:20:23,162 --> 00:20:39,731
[SPEAKER_00]: So... and that really just comes down to having an attitude of sense of ownership and sense of control over things that you realize were never yours to own, were never in your control, and could never even be possible to own or control.

134
00:20:40,992 --> 00:20:51,278
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's like... and that's when the mind would kind of let go of that... let go of that anxiety and let go of that resistance, because it would see how futile and useless it is.

135
00:20:53,718 --> 00:20:58,204
[SPEAKER_00]: It makes no difference to the situation anyway.

136
00:20:59,325 --> 00:21:02,048
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it's really the cause of suffering.

137
00:21:02,669 --> 00:21:09,598
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, that's something that you can only see if you've been developing your mind along the right lines.

138
00:21:09,838 --> 00:21:20,691
[SPEAKER_00]: If not, it's like literally your whole mindset is set against it, is set in maintaining the ignorance of it, is maintaining the impossibility of seeing it.

